== Repentance Must Go With Forgiveness

#smallcaps[It] is clear from the text we have just quoted that repentance is bound up with the forgiveness of sins. In Acts 5:31 we read that Jesus is “exalted to give repentance and forgiveness of sins.” These two blessings come from that sacred hand which was once nailed to the tree, but is now raised to glory. Repentance and forgiveness are riveted together by the eternal purpose of God. What God has joined together, let no man separate.

Repentance must go with remission, and you will see that it is so if you think about the matter a little. #emph[Pardon of sin cannot be given to an impenitent sinner]\; that would confirm him in his evil ways, and teach him to think little of evil. If the Lord were to say, “You love sin, and live in it, and you are going from bad to worse, but all the same, I forgive you,” that would proclaim a horrible license for iniquity. The foundations of social order would be removed, and moral anarchy would follow. I cannot tell what countless evils would certainly follow if you could separate repentance and forgiveness, and overlook the sin while the sinner remained as fond of it as ever. In the very nature of things, if we believe in #emph[the holiness of God], it must be so: if we continue in our sin, and will not repent of it, we cannot be forgiven, but must reap the consequences of our stubbornness. In the infinite goodness of God, we are promised that if we will forsake our sins, confessing them, and will by faith accept the grace provided in Christ Jesus, God is faithful and just to forgive us our sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness. But as long as God lives, there can be no promise of mercy to those who continue in their evil ways, and refuse to acknowledge their wrongdoing. Surely no rebel can expect the King to pardon his treason while he remains in open revolt. No one can be so foolish as to imagine that the Judge of all the earth will put away our sins if we refuse to put them away ourselves.

Moreover, it must be so for #emph[the completeness of divine mercy]. A mercy that could forgive the sin and yet let the sinner live in it would be a thin and shallow mercy. It would be a lopsided and deformed mercy, lame in one of its feet, and withered in one of its hands. Which do you think is the greater privilege: cleansing from the guilt of sin, or deliverance from the power of sin? I will not try to weigh in the scales two mercies so surpassing. Neither of them could have come to us apart from the precious blood of Jesus. But it seems to me that to be delivered from the dominion of sin, to be made holy, to be made like God, must be counted the greater of the two, if a comparison has to be drawn. To be forgiven is an immeasurable favor. We make this one of the first notes of our psalm of praise: “He who forgives all your iniquities.” But if we could be forgiven, and then be permitted to love sin, to run riot in iniquity, and to wallow in lust, what would be the use of such a forgiveness? Might it not turn out to be a poisoned sweet, which would most effectually destroy us? To be washed, and yet to lie in the mud; to be pronounced clean, and yet to have the leprosy white on one’s brow, would be the sheerest mockery of mercy. What good is it to bring the man out of his tomb if you leave him dead? Why lead him into the light if he is still blind? We thank God that he who forgives our iniquities also heals our diseases. He who washes us from the stains of the past also lifts us out of the foul ways of the present, and keeps us from falling in the future. We must joyfully accept both repentance and remission; they cannot be separated. The inheritance of the covenant is one and indivisible, and must not be parceled out. To divide the work of grace would be to cut the living child in two, and those who would allow this have no real claim to it.

I will ask you who are seeking the Lord: would you be satisfied with one of these mercies alone? Would it content you, my reader, if God forgave your sin and then allowed you to be as worldly and wicked as before? Oh, no: the spirit made alive is more afraid of sin itself than of its penalty. The cry of your heart is not, “Who will rescue me from punishment?” but, “What a wretched man I am! Who will rescue me from this body of death? Who will enable me to live above temptation, and to become holy, even as God is holy?” Since the union of repentance with remission agrees with the desires of a gracious heart, and since it is necessary for the completeness of salvation, and for holiness’ sake, you may rest assured that it stands.

Repentance and forgiveness are joined together #emph[in the experience of all believers]. There never yet was a person who sincerely repented of sin with believing repentance who was not forgiven; and on the other hand, there never was a person forgiven who had not repented of his sin. I do not hesitate to say that under the vault of heaven there never was, there is not, and there never will be, any case of sin being washed away, unless at the same time the heart was led to repentance and faith in Christ. Hatred of sin and a sense of pardon come into the soul together, and remain together as long as we live.

#emph[These two things act and react on each other]: the man who is forgiven therefore repents; and the man who repents is also most certainly forgiven. Remember first that forgiveness leads to repentance. As we sing in Hart’s words:

#quote[“Law and terrors do but harden, \ All the while they work alone; \ But a sense of blood-bought pardon \ Soon dissolves a heart of stone.”]

When we are sure that we are forgiven, then we abhor iniquity; and I suppose that when faith grows into full assurance, so that we are certain beyond a doubt that the blood of Jesus has washed us whiter than snow, it is then that repentance reaches its greatest height. Repentance grows as faith grows. Make no mistake about it: repentance is not a thing of days and weeks, a temporary penance to be gotten over as fast as possible! No; it is the grace of a lifetime, like faith itself. God’s little children repent, and so do the young men and the fathers. Repentance is the inseparable companion of faith. All the while that we walk by faith and not by sight, the tear of repentance glistens in the eye of faith. That is not true repentance which does not come from faith in Jesus, and that is not true faith in Jesus which is not tinged with repentance. Faith and repentance, like conjoined twins, are joined together in one life. The more we believe in the forgiving love of Christ, the more we repent; and the more we repent of sin and hate evil, the more we rejoice in the fullness of the absolution which Jesus is exalted to give. You will never value pardon unless you feel repentance; and you will never taste repentance at its deepest until you know that you are pardoned. It may seem a strange thing, but so it is — the bitterness of repentance and the sweetness of pardon blend in the flavor of every gracious life, and make up an incomparable happiness.

These two covenant gifts assure us of each other. If I know that I repent, I know that I am forgiven. How am I to know that I am forgiven unless I also know that I have turned from my former sinful course? To be a believer is to be a penitent. Faith and repentance are only two spokes in the same wheel, two handles of the same plow. Repentance has been well described as a heart broken #emph[for] sin, and #emph[from] sin; and it may just as well be spoken of as turning and returning. It is a change of mind of the most thorough and radical kind, and it comes with sorrow for the past and a resolve to do better in the future.

#quote[“Repentance is to leave \ The sins we loved before; \ And show that we in earnest grieve, \ By doing so no more.”]

Now, when that is so, we may be certain that we are forgiven; for the Lord never made a heart to be broken for sin and broken from sin without pardoning it. If, on the other hand, we are enjoying pardon through the blood of Jesus, and are justified by faith, and have peace with God through Jesus Christ our Lord, we know that our repentance and faith are of the right kind.

Do not regard your repentance as the cause of your remission, but as its companion. Do not expect to be able to repent until you see the grace of our Lord Jesus, and his readiness to blot out your sin. Keep these blessed things in their places, and see them in their relation to each other. They are the Jachin and Boaz of a saving experience; I mean that they are like Solomon’s two great pillars which stood at the front of the house of the Lord, and formed a majestic entrance to the holy place. No man comes to God rightly unless he passes between the pillars of repentance and remission. The rainbow of covenant grace has been displayed on your heart in all its beauty when the light of full forgiveness has shone on the teardrops of repentance. Repentance of sin and faith in divine pardon are the warp and woof of the fabric of real conversion. By these marks you will know a true Israelite.

To come back to the Scripture we are meditating on: both forgiveness and repentance flow from the same source, and are given by the same Savior. The Lord Jesus in his glory gives both to the same people. You are not to find either the remission or the repentance anywhere else. Jesus has both ready, and he is prepared to give them now, and to give them most freely to all who will accept them from his hands. Let it never be forgotten that Jesus gives all that is needed for our salvation. It is highly important that all who seek mercy should remember this. Faith is as much the gift of God as is the Savior on whom that faith relies. Repentance of sin is as truly the work of grace as the making of an atonement by which sin is blotted out. Salvation, from first to last, is of grace alone. You will not misunderstand me. It is not the Holy Spirit who repents. He has never done anything for which he should repent. If he could repent, it would not meet the case; we must repent of our own sin ourselves, or we are not saved from its power. It is not the Lord Jesus Christ who repents. What should he repent of? We ourselves repent with the full consent of every faculty of our mind. The will, the affections, the emotions, all work together most heartily in the blessed act of repentance for sin; and yet behind all that is our personal act there is a secret, holy influence which melts the heart, gives contrition, and produces a complete change. The Spirit of God enlightens us to see what sin is, and so makes it loathsome in our eyes. The Spirit of God also turns us toward holiness, makes us heartily appreciate, love, and desire it, and so gives us the impulse by which we are led on from stage to stage of sanctification. The Spirit of God works in us to will and to do according to God’s good pleasure. To that good Spirit let us submit ourselves at once, that he may lead us to Jesus, who will freely give us the double blessing of repentance and remission, according to the riches of his grace.

